--expect
shape=0,0,0 cab=2,2,2 absent=-,- span=2 resume=1 from=3,3,-,- empty=0,2,6 long=- utf8=2,1 ropeneedle=0 needleflat=1 flattens=1 gas=6,6 starved=-
--expect-why
DERIVED BY HAND from `ropefind.kin`, counting the offsets off each fixture
haystack byte by byte and code point by code point. Nothing was read off a
run. Every index below is a CODE-POINT index, which is what `s-index-of`
returns; `-` is NIL.

THE HAYSTACKS. Four spellings of the same six bytes "abcabc", so that any
field whose three numbers differ is a SHAPE dependence and nothing else:

  hf  one flat leaf          [abcabc]
  h3  three flat leaves      [a][bc][abc]
  hi  a flat, an INLINE, a flat   [a][bca][bc]    -- the inline has no address
  hb  three flat leaves      [ab][ca][bc]         -- used for `span`

`shape=0,0,0` -- "abc" begins at 0 in all three. `cab=2,2,2` -- "cab" is the
bytes at 2, 3, 4. Counted off "abcabc": a0 b1 c2 a3 b4 c5, so c-a-b starts at
2. The three answers must be IDENTICAL: `hi`'s match at 2 begins inside an
inline leaf whose `p0 + nn` = 1 + 3 exceeds its three bytes, so it takes
`match-at`'s cursor path rather than the `run-eq` memcmp, and kin's own note
says an inline leaf has no address. Two spellings of the same string that
answered differently would be the whole bug this file exists to prevent.

`absent=-,-` -- "zz" is not in "abcabc". NIL, not a number: kin's doc says
there is no -1 to return because `I32` is unsigned, so a search that tried to
signal failure numerically would print something like 4294967295 here.

`span=2` -- over [ab][ca][bc], "cab" is bytes 2, 3, 4, which sit in leaf 2
(bytes 2-3) and leaf 3 (byte 4). The match therefore crosses a leaf boundary
mid-needle, which is exactly `match-at`'s duplicate-cursor path.

`resume=1` -- haystack [a][ab] = "aab", needle "ab". The FIRST 'a' starts a
match that compares 'a' (ok), crosses into the next leaf, compares 'a' against
'b' and FAILS. The search must then resume at the very next byte, find 'a' at
byte 1 and match "ab" there: the answer is 1. This is what `walk-dup` is for
  -- the failed match moved a COPY of the cursor into leaf 2, and if it had
moved the outer walk instead, byte 1 would never be examined and the answer
would be `-`. 1 and `-` are the two candidate answers, and only one of them
can be produced by a correct resume.

`from=3,3,-,-` over h3 = [a][bc][abc], needle "abc":

  from=1  the next occurrence at code point >= 1 is 3
  from=3  exactly AT an occurrence, so 3 -- `>=`, not `>`
  from=4  nothing left, so `-`
  from=99 past the end, so `-`

THE FIRST IS THE ONE KIN'S COMMENT NAMES. Seeking to `from` is a descent:
`rope-byte-of-cp h3 1` = 1, and the walk then skips leaves WHOLLY before that
offset by length. Leaf [a] has base 0 and length 1, so base + n = 1 <= 1 and it
is skipped without a byte being read. Leaf [bc] has base 1, and `base <=
startbyte` (1 <= 1) fires, setting p = startbyte - base = 0 and cp = 1. With
`<` there instead of `<=` the leaf that begins exactly at the seek point gets
no `cp`, the walk believes it is at code point 0, and the answer comes out 2
instead of 3 -- the exact off-by-one the comment records. `from=3` is the
other side of the same test: leaf [bc] is skipped by length (base 1 + n 2 = 3
<= 3), leaf [abc] takes the jump with p = 0, cp = 3.

`empty=0,2,6` -- an EMPTY needle is found WHERE THE SEARCH STARTS, clamped to
the code-point count. h3 holds 6 code points, so from 0 gives 0, from 2 gives
2, and from 99 gives 6 -- NOT `-`, and not 99. This is Clojure's
`(str/index-of "abc" "" 100)` = 3, and kin's comment records that the three
runtimes gave three different answers here depending on whether the haystack
was ASCII.

`long=-` -- a nine-byte needle in a six-byte haystack. The cursor path runs
off the end of the tree: `walk-next` answers nil, and `match-at` must treat
that as a FAILED match rather than a finished one. An implementation that fell
out of the loop with `ok` still true would report a match at 0.

`utf8=2,1` -- haystack "a" + U+03B2 + "c" = bytes 61 CE B2 63, SPLIT AS
[61 CE][B2 63] so a continuation byte is the first byte of a leaf.

  "c"    is byte 3 but CODE POINT 2 -- a, beta, c. Answering 3 is the
         byte offset, which is what a search that never converted would say.
  "beta" is code point 1.

The counting rule is kin's: a byte is a code-point start unless
`b & 0xC0 == 0x80`. Walking it: 61 starts (cp 0), CE starts (cp 1), B2 is a
continuation and starts nothing, 63 starts (cp 2). This haystack is NOT ASCII,
so the probe takes the counting branch rather than the `cp += hit - p`
shortcut -- both branches exist in kin and only this fixture exercises the
slow one.

`ropeneedle=0 needleflat=1 flattens=1` IS THE CLAIM THE WHOLE FILE IS ABOUT.
A ROPE needle is flattened -- it is bounded by the pattern -- so the delta
across that one call is exactly 1. And the running total over EVERY call above
is also 1: every haystack in this probe was a rope, and not one of them was
ever materialised. `flattens=2` would mean a haystack went through
`s-flatten`, which is the 897-steps-versus-21 regression named in the header.
The needle still has to be FOUND after flattening, hence `ropeneedle=0` beside
it -- flattening the wrong thing and flattening nothing are both visible here.

`gas=6,6` -- THE SAME BYTES IN TWO SHAPES MUST COST THE SAME. Ticks charge 0
(kin passes 0 to `charge-tick`; the tick only tests the limit), so every unit
is a byte actually looked at. Searching "abcabc" for "cab":

  hf, one leaf:   leaf-find jumps the run "ab" to the 'c'        2
                  the byte at p is examined                      1
                  match-at: p0+nn = 5 <= 6, so the memcmp path
                  charges the needle's length                    3
                                                                 = 6
  h3, [a][bc][abc]:
                  leaf [a]: no 'c', run of 1 charged             1
                  leaf [bc]: 'c' found at 1, run of 1 charged    1
                  the byte at p examined                         1
                  match-at: 1 + 3 > 2, so the CURSOR path, one
                  charge per byte compared, 3 compared, 3 matched 3
                                                                 = 6

Both 6, by two different routes -- one memcmp, one byte-at-a-time across two
leaf boundaries. Kin's own comment records the number that a per-LEAF tick
produced instead: `gas=4,5`, unequal, which is the shape-dependence this
field refuses.

`starved=-` -- and this one is PINNED IN MEANING but weak as a check, which
is worth saying rather than dressing up. With `limit = 3` the scan over hf for
"zz" answers NIL. But "zz" is genuinely absent, so `-` is also the answer with
no limit at all (see `absent`), and the fixture's `charge-tick` only tests the
limit when `i & 63 == 0` -- this search ticks twice, so the limit is never
re-examined. The value is correct and derived; what it demonstrates is that a
limit does not make the search CRASH or report a spurious hit, not that it cut
the scan short.
